1
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Just beyond your dreams lives a secret world. Where every tree is a home. Every
sound is a song. And humans only exist in fairy tales. Until now...

2
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:42,000
FernGully: The Last Rainforest is a family, fantasy,
animation film directed by Bill Kroyer.

3
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:30,000
When a sprite named Crysta shrinks a human boy, Zak, down to her size, he vows to help the magical fairy folk stop
a greedy logging company from destroying their home: the pristine rainforest known as FernGully.

4
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:18,000
Zak and his new friends fight to defend FernGully from lumberjacks — and the vengeful
spirit they accidentally unleash after chopping down a magic tree.

5
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:06,000
Crysta (voice), played by Samantha Mathis.

6
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:54,000
Zak Young (voice), played by Jonathan Ward.

7
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:42,000
Pips (voice), played by Christian Slater.

8
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:30,000
Batty Koda (voice), played by Robin Williams.

9
00:34:12,000 --> 00:34:18,000
Hexxus (voice), played by Tim Curry.

10
00:38:00,000 --> 00:38:06,000
Magi Lune (voice), played by Grace Zabriskie.

11
00:41:48,000 --> 00:41:54,000
FernGully: The Last Rainforest is a 1992 animated musical fantasy film
directed by Bill Kroyer in his feature directorial debut.

12
00:45:36,000 --> 00:45:42,000
Scripted by Jim Cox and adapted from the "FernGully" stories by Diana Young, the film is an Australian and American venture
produced by Kroyer Films, Inc., Youngheart Productions, and FAI Films, and distributed by 20th Century Fox.

13
00:49:24,000 --> 00:49:30,000
It stars the voices of Samantha Mathis, Christian Slater, Jonathan
Ward, Robin Williams, Tim Curry, and Grace Zabriskie.

14
00:53:12,000 --> 00:53:18,000
The film is set in an Australian rainforest inhabited by fairies, including Crysta, who
accidentally shrinks a young logger named Zak to the size of a fairy.

15
00:57:00,000 --> 00:57:06,000
Together, they rally the fairies and the animals of the rainforest to protect their
home from the loggers and Hexxus, a malevolent pollution entity.

16
01:00:48,000 --> 01:00:54,000
Wayne Young, the film's producer, said that the film was "blatantly
environmental", although he made an effort to avoid "preaching".

17
01:04:36,000 --> 01:04:42,000
FernGully was released in North America on April 10, 1992, to mainly positive reviews, and was generally
considered a moderate financial success at both the box office and in home video sales.

18
01:08:24,000 --> 01:08:30,000
In 1998, it was followed by a direct-to-video sequel, FernGully 2: The Magical
Rescue, although none of the original voice cast reprised their roles.

19
01:12:12,000 --> 01:12:18,000
The film explores themes of pollution, fairy, musical, bat, environment.
